---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What CyberAware is, why it's built the way it is, and an explicit statement of what data is (and isn't) collected."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-(--color-text)">
      About CyberAware
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-(--color-text)">What this is</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-(--color-text-muted)">
          CyberAware teaches people to recognize and respond to common cyber
          attacks by actually doing it, not by reading about it. Every
          simulation walks through a real attack pattern step by step; every
          tool analyzes something you give it and explains exactly what it
          found; every quiz scores a real judgment call, with an explanation
          either way. The goal is to leave you able to spot these attacks in
          real life &mdash; and, for anyone considering a cybersecurity role,
          to build the kind of practical judgment an entry-level position
          actually expects, not just facts to recite.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-(--color-text)">Why it's built this way</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-(--color-text-muted)">
          The topics covered here aren't arbitrary. They're grounded in
          published breach data and established security frameworks &mdash;
          including the Verizon Data Breach Investigations Report, the MITRE
          ATT&CK framework, the OWASP Top 10, and the domains covered by the
          CompTIA Security+ certification &mdash; so the difficulty and
          priority of each topic reflects how often it actually happens and
          how much it actually matters, not a guess. Content is organized
          into five domains (Email &amp; Message Security, Human &amp; Social
          Engineering, Network &amp; Wireless Security, Malware &amp;
          Ransomware, and Identity &amp; Access) across five difficulty
          levels, from single obvious red flags up to ambiguous, realistic
          scenarios. The <a
            href="/roadmap/"
            class="text-(--color-accent) underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-(--color-accent)"
          >Roadmap</a> lays this whole structure out and tracks what you've completed
          in each one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-(--color-text)">No data is ever collected</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-(--color-text-muted)">
          This is a fully static site: no backend server, no accounts, no
          sign-ups, and no network calls of any kind once the page has
          loaded. That's not a policy promise &mdash; it's a structural fact
          about how the site is built, since there's nowhere for your data to
          go even if it wanted to.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-(--color-text-muted)">
          <li>
            Every tool &mdash; the Password Strength Analyzer, the Credential
            Reuse Check, the Phishing Email Scanner, the URL Safety Checker
            &mdash; analyzes what you type or paste entirely inside your own
            browser. None of it is ever sent anywhere, logged, or stored
            outside your device.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your progress (which simulations, tools, and quizzes you've
            completed, and your quiz scores) is saved only in your browser's
            local storage, so it survives a page reload but never leaves
            your device. You can review it any time on the <a
              href="/progress/"
              class="text-(--color-accent) underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-(--color-accent)"
            >My Progress</a> page, and clear it whenever you want.
          </li>
          <li>
            There is no analytics, no tracking script, and no third-party
            embed of any kind.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-(--color-text)">Educational use only</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-(--color-text-muted)">
          No real attacks are ever performed by this site. Every simulated
          brand, company, and person (like "SecurePay" or "Meridian
          Bookkeeping") is fictional. When a tool asks you to try a practice
          password or a fake account label, don't type something you
          actually use elsewhere &mdash; use something similar in structure
          instead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
